I've written 48 tests in `tests/private/`. All 48 fail against the current `not implemented` interface, and `npm run build` reports no errors.

To check that the suite isn't wrong in the other direction, I wrote a reference implementation in the scratchpad (nothing in `src/` changed). It passes both the public suite and all 48 private tests. I then made five deliberately flawed versions of it; the private suite caught every one:

- **No locking:** 9 private tests fail, including lost updates when different codes are redeemed at once.
- **Only one space and one dash stripped from typed codes:** the public suite passes, 5 private tests fail.
- **"Already has access" whenever the contributor has any access at all:** 8 private tests fail.
- **Duplicate check only within the same task:** the public suite passes, 2 private tests fail.
- **Revoking a revoked code again withdraws the user's current access:** caught.

**What the suite covers:**
- All 12 scenarios have tests (happy-1 to 4, boundary-1 to 5, unhappy-1 and 2, concurrency-1), and each public test has at least one private test extending it, named in a comment above.
- **Shortcuts targeted:**
  - more than 15 codes;
  - repeats checked against codes for other tasks, against used, revoked and expired codes, and redrawn more than once;
  - codes typed with mixed case, spaces between every character, or all three variations at once;
  - each code expiring from its own creation time;
  - a code refused with "already has access" that can later regain the task;
  - races with up to five contenders, many store delay patterns, a store that reads late, and contenders typing the same code differently.

**Left untested because the spec doesn't say:**
- which reason wins when more than one applies (for example, used and expired);
- what revoking a code that was never made does;
- whether `revoke` accepts lowercase or dashed input;
- the order of `tasksFor` (always compared sorted);
- which letter each random draw maps to (only the alphabet and that the same draws give the same codes).

**Spec vs interface:** the comment on `revoke` in `src/index.ts` says it "Throws if the code has been used". The spec, and the public suite, require revoking a used code to succeed and withdraw its access. I followed the spec.

**Housekeeping:** I deleted the `.js` files my builds created outside `tests/private/`. There's also an empty `tests/private/.claude/` folder, which the tool harness created when the working directory moved there for a moment. I didn't create it and left it in place; it only matters if the grader objects to anything there besides `.test.ts` files.

48 tests, in 3 files.

Open in the spec:
- `[boundary-5] revoking an already revoked code changes nothing, so regained access is kept` checks that revoking the code a second time succeeds without an error. The spec only says this "changes nothing", and the interface comment says `revoke` throws for a code that has been used. Whether the second revoke may throw is open, so my tests accept either.
